  "response": "## PR Review: `src/assets/hero-banner.png`\n\n**Finding:** `crime_00011` — Oversized Raster\n**File size:** 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB), format PNG\n**Severity:** low (threshold ladder: low >= 200 KB, medium >= 500 KB, high >= 1 MB)\n\n### Verdict: Request changes, don't block\n\n253.9 KB for a hero banner isn't catastrophic, but it's the single heaviest asset on the landing page and sits directly in the LCP critical path. A PNG hero will be the largest contentful paint element — every KB here costs real CWV score. This is fixable in minutes, so no reason to merge it as-is.\n\n### Recommended transformation\n\n**Target dimensions:** Resize to the actual CSS render width. A typical full-bleed hero renders at ~1440px wide; serve a 1x asset at 1440px and let `srcset` handle 2x (2880px) only for high-DPI screens. If the current PNG is wider than 2880px, you're shipping pixels nobody sees.\n\n**Format:** Re-encode to **WebP**, not AVIF, for the primary source:\n\n- WebP gives ~70-80% size reduction over PNG for photographic content — this 254 KB image should land around 50-80 KB.\n- AVIF compresses ~20% better than WebP, but its decode time is meaningfully slower, which matters for an above-the-fold LCP element.\n- Browser support for WebP is effectively universal (~97%+); AVIF still has gaps in older Safari and some embedded browsers.\n\nIf you want both, use a `<picture>` element with AVIF as the first `<source>` and WebP as fallback — browsers that can decode AVIF fast enough will benefit, others get WebP. Keep the PNG out of the final bundle entirely.\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source srcset=\"hero-banner.avif\" type=\"image/avif\" />\n  <source srcset=\"hero-banner.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"hero-banner.webp\" alt=\"...\" width=\"1440\" height=\"...\" loading=\"eager\" fetchpriority=\"high\" />\n</picture>\n```\n\nSince this is the LCP element: `loading=\"eager\"` and `fetchpriority=\"high\"`, not `lazy`.",
